# Character set a translated cartridge stores text in — KEYBOARD LAYOUT.
#
# The name-entry grid assigns codes to cells sequentially (katakana page:
# 0x01+cell in rows of 15,15,15,10; hiragana page: 0x38+cell), and each cell
# both SHOWS the font glyph of its code and TYPES that code. So this table is
# designed around the keyboard: page 1 spells the upper case, page 2 the lower
# case, and with every line of the game translated the kana codes are free.
#
# Do NOT regenerate this file with `slots --write` — that command picks codes
# by kana frequency, which only matters while Japanese text still displays.
#
# The FIRST code listed for a character is what the encoder writes; later
# codes are keyboard aliases (extra cells that show and type the same glyph).

# -- native Latin block: canonical for capitals, digits and punctuation ------
9E=A
9F=B
A0=C
A1=D
A2=E
A3=F
A4=G
A5=H
A6=I
A7=J
A8=K
A9=L
AA=M
AB=N
AC=O
AD=P
AE=Q
AF=R
B0=S
B1=T
B2=U
B3=V
B4=W
B5=X
B6=Y
B7=Z
B8=?
B9=!
BB=0
BC=1
BD=2
BE=3
BF=4
C0=5
C1=6
C2=7
C3=8
C4=9
C8=.
CB=/
CD=-
CE=+
D7='
D8=(
D9=)
DB=
DD="

# -- symbols the game already draws ------------------------------------------
C5=△
C6=▲
C7=×
CC=▼
CF=[<3]
D0=♀
D1=♂
D2=↑
D3=↓
D4=→
D5=←
D6=·
DA=±
DC=[note]
DE=°

